==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ʌmp}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|slumps|slumping|slumped}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[collapse]] [[heavily]] or [[helplessly]].
#: ''Exhausted, he '''slumped''' down onto the sofa.''
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[decline]] or [[fall off]] in [[activity]] or [[performance]].
#: ''Real estate prices '''slumped''' during the recession.''
#*{{quote-news
|year=2011
|date=October 29
|author=Phil McNulty
|title=Chelsea   3 - 5   Arsenal
|work=BBC Sport
|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/15413593.stm
|page=
|passage=The Gunners captain demonstrated his importance to the team by taking his tally to an outstanding 28 goals in 27 Premier League games as Chelsea '''slumped''' again after their shock defeat at QPR last week.}}
# {{context|intransitive|lang=en}} To [[slouch]] or [[droop]].
# {{context|transitive|lang=en}} To [[lump]]; to throw together messily.
#* Sir W. Hamilton
#*: These different groups {{...}} are exclusively '''slumped''' together under that sense.
# To fall or sink suddenly through or in, when walking on a surface, as on thawing snow or ice, a bog, etc.
#* Barrow
#*: The latter walk on a bottomless quag, into which unawares they may '''slump'''.

